#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186074968 :tutorial/content "Remember that we can place equivalent ratios in a ratio table, like the one below.\n\n| $\\color{blue}x$ | $\\color{red}y$ |\n| --- | ---: |\n| $\\color{blue}1$ | $\\color{red}2$ |\n| $\\color{blue}2$ | $\\color{red}4$ |\n| $\\color{blue}4$ | $\\color{red}8$ |\n\nBy setting the values in the left column as $\\color{blue}x$-coordinates and the values in the right column as the corresponding $\\color{red}y$-coordinates, we can graph the points to represent a relationship between the ratios.\n\nIn this case, we have the following ordered pairs: $({\\color{blue}1},{\\color{red}2})$, $({\\color{blue}2},{\\color{red}4})$, $({\\color{blue}4},{\\color{red}8})$.\n\nPlotting these points on a coordinate plane, we get the following graph:\n\n![](images/0c/0c4abb8d462730ed9b532feeca1e0a800f5376a978bfc799afb9d9a927ff310d.png)\n\nConnecting the points gives us a straight line! Plotting equivalent ratios will *always* give a straight line. Furthermore, the straight line will *always* pass through the origin $(0,0)$." :tutorial/id #uuid "05d7bd91-e8b4-5ada-936f-6036f766cf23" :tutorial/math-academy-id 2053 :tutorial/title "Introduction"}]}
